# Merging an exposure bracket, then fitting it onto a display.
#
# Drop your bracket on one palette row -- hold ALT while dropping to make them
# a group in one go -- then right-click the group and "Sort by filename" so the
# order is what you expect.
#
# merge_hdr reads each frame's shutter, aperture and ISO from its EXIF and
# divides them out, so every frame contributes the same radiance where it is
# well exposed. The result is scene-linear with real headroom: on a seven-frame
# bracket it reaches 900-odd, with the midtones near 2.0.
#
# tonemap is what makes that displayable. It MEASURES the image -- a merge's
# absolute scale depends on the shutter speeds used, so no fixed anchor could
# be right -- puts the median on middle grey, and rolls the highlights off. It
# is a separate stage rather than part of the merge on purpose: any
# scene-linear image can need it, and swapping in a different operator should
# be a one-line change.

frames = image("bracket")
merged = merge_hdr(frames)

# exposure moves the midtones (1.0 = middle grey), shoulder sets how much
# highlight headroom is kept above them. Watch the status line: it reports the
# scale it chose and how far the highlights reach.
shown = tonemap(merged)

# Ordinary develop on top, now that the data is in a sane range. The sliders
# mean what they mean on a raw.
final = basic_adjust(shown)

display(merged, "linear")
display(shown,  "tonemapped")
display(final,  "developed")
